t areas  weapons ammunition and intelligence  weapons have been absolutely essential  for example in the most recent harkiv  offensive Ukrainian officials tell us  that  anti-radiation missiles provided by  America have been vital to taking out  Russian air defense radars offensives  are also incredibly demanding in terms  of the ammunition that you use you end  up using far more bullets shells  um Rockets than you would when you're on  the defense and so maintaining a supply  of those uh vital shells rounds bullets  has been key to allowing Ukraine to go  on the offensive Western intelligence to  Ukraine has been crucial in identifying  Russian positions identifying Russian  movements and it's been intense  throughout this campaign but I think it  has stepped up in the last few weeks and  intelligence was really critical to  allowing Ukraine to catch Russia by  surprise in Hakeem as well  the reaction from the Kremlin  um has been in some ways very typical  that is when things are not going well  the Kremlin keeps actually silent  um it wasn't covering it all together  there were reports of Russian retreat  they called it regrouping rather than  running away from the enemy uh Vladimir  Putin himself tried to distance himself  from  um any uh commentary or from the war  itself Vladimir Putin spend that day  opening ferries will in the exhibition  sent in the theme park that actually  broke down very soon after he opened it  but even so it's clear that the anger is  building up and the anger not just  necessarily from the uh anti-war  part of the population from the Liberals  because they really have been uh  gagged and you know closed down  um and censored but actually very  interesting there is a lot of anger  coming from the right-wing nationalists  who are saying more radical uh war is  needed Russia should go further Russia  should escalate uh and they're starting  to blame Uh Russian command they're  starting to blame corruption in the  Russian  um Armed Forces uh and this is not  looking good for Putin  foreign  that could produce very serious anger  um particularly among the younger people  who would have to go and serve Russia is  a country with a demographic of 1.6  children per family we know from the  opinion polls that  um  the support for continuing this war is  actually waning particularly at the  younger part of the demographic so it  could have very unpleasant political  Fallout from him I don't know what  Shashank things about the actual how  much would it give him in terms of the  military Advantage at this stage in the  campaign I think the important thing to  remember is uh more soldiers more young  men isn't the end of Putin's problem he  has to find a way to train them to  organize them and to equip them and so  mobilization May yield him a bigger Army  but it will take many many months  perhaps over a year to fully feel its  effects and even then it won't  necessarily be an incredibly combat  effective Force  foreign  constant concerns over potential nuclear  escalation throughout this campaign and  in my opinion those concerns have been  somewhat over egged or overrated yes I  think there is apprehension that if  there is a sudden collapse of Russia's  armies and particularly if the Ukrainian  Army begins to threaten territory that  Russia considers to be a sovereign part  of Russia but I think we're some way  away from this at this point in time and  the risks of that course of action would  be enormous perhaps riskier than  grinding out the current path  foreign  Victory Is defined in many ways by  different participants in this conflict  but if we choose to Define it as Ukraine  restoring the borders that prevailed on  February 23rd the day before Vladimir  Putin began his invasion I think what  the significance of this offensive is is  not that it means Victory is around the  corner or that it means it means it's  imminent what it does is something in  some ways as important it shows us that  Victory Is Possible and it shows those  who doubted that Ukraine could dislodge  Russia that this was a a forlorn hope it  shows them that the war is winnable and  that message I think is of huge  inestimable value both for ukrainians  and for the International Community  supporting them through a winter that's  going to be difficult uh tough with high  gas prices with our cost of living  crisis it shows them that Victory is  also possible in pure military terms I  think Shashank is right I think the  retreat Now sort of beating Russians  back to the 20 third of February lines  is absolutely possible  and I think the world sees this and I  think Ukraine sees it Ukrainian goals  have changed in this campaign that  they're no longer going to be satisfied  with the restoring the status quo as was  on the 23rd of February they actually  want all of their territory back and  they think they can get it and they also  want Crimea back  which Russia annexed in 2014.  for Putin uh losing this battle  uh in the way that Ukraine defines it  would be the end of his regime uh would  be the end of the political system it  could be the end of Russia as we know it  at the moment Putin's legitimacy is  entirely based on his success on Victory  not just his actually this legitimacy of  all Russian rulers has been tied with  victories and successes  and he was considered to be lucky uh he  was always considered to be victorious  um so this is a big setback  um his legitimacy will suffer uh as a  result but Putin has been in power for  22 years  it's very hard for people to imagine  what Russia would look like without him  it's very hard at the moment to imagine  this this regime uh can actually  collapse uh suddenly and unexpectedly as  the Soviet one did as one  um  Anthropologist wrote about the Soviet  regime everything was forever until it  was no more when that moment comes we  can't say but surely this military  defeat uh brings one step closer to that  moment to read more of our cove